{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794370865702,"sessionID":"ses_e37366a2affe5Df7hppu0PfBho","part":{"id":"prt_1c8d68a0a001P0bdTkRb2kQE2e","messageID":"msg_1c8d6752a001NN7nPfnfBKHkhs","sessionID":"ses_e37366a2affe5Df7hppu0PfBho","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794370865702,"sessionID":"ses_e37366a2affe5Df7hppu0PfBho","part":{"id":"prt_1c8d68a0d001xceUPj6yQ0BEUA","messageID":"msg_1c8d6752a001NN7nPfnfBKHkhs","sessionID":"ses_e37366a2affe5Df7hppu0PfBho","type":"text","text":"<think>The server.log wasn't overwritten and the data dir wasn't removed - it seems the whole command was killed by the timeout before executing? Actually the issue: `pkill -f \"port=8011\"` — the pkill pattern may match the bash command itself (the command line contains \"port=8011\"), killing the shell. That's why the whole command hangs/fails.\n\nLet me avoid pkill matching itself. Use a different approach: write a start script and use a PID file.\n\nActually the shell tool terminated the command after 120s — because `setsid nohup ... &` with the shell still waiting? Previously the same pattern worked (port 8011 started fine) but the command also timed out at 120s. So the background job keeps the shell alive. The trick is to redirect properly and disown.\n\nLet me just use a helper script approach with `&` and explicit `disown`, and importantly the shell tool waits for output streams to close. Using `> log 2>&1 < /dev/null &` should detach stdout/stderr. Hmm, but the earlier one still timed out.\n\nActually earlier, the first command with `( ... &)` completed successfully within timeout (the one that printed `{\"status\": \"ok\"}` then timed out). The timeout happened because the tool waits for the process group. Let me try `setsid` with `disown` and redirect.\n\nBetter: use a wrapper script and `nohup ... &` then `exit 0`? The tool might be waiting for all children. Let me try running the server with `setsid` and fully detaching stdin/stdout/stderr, and then in a separate command check.\n\nLet me first check if the 8012 server actually is running (maybe it started but curl failed because data dir removal happened after?). Let me check processes.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794370865677,"end":1794370865697}}}
